CH = dict(num=9, title="Cell: The Building Block of Life", tag="Biology", theme="Cell structure, organelles, osmosis, cell division, cell theory",
 intro="All living things are made of cells. This chapter shows how cells are studied with microscopes, what a cell is made of (membrane, wall, cytoplasm, nucleus and organelles), how water moves in and out by osmosis, how cells divide by mitosis and meiosis, and how the Cell Theory unites all of biology.",
 big_questions=["Where does a cell come from?", "How is the cell the structural and functional unit of life?", "How does a cell multiply?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "9.1 Origin of Life and Levels of Organisation"),
 ("ul", ["Scientists widely accept that life began in water, possibly in small pools with changing conditions, such as **hot springs**. The hot springs of **Puga Valley (Ladakh)** stay near boiling point even in cold air, resembling early Earth (about 3.5 billion years ago). They hold heat-loving, single-celled bacteria called **thermophiles**.", "Researchers of the Birbal Sahni Institute of Palaeosciences (Lucknow) found **calcium carbonate** deposits forming rapidly around them. Such deposits may have shielded early organic molecules and helped form the first protective membrane.", "**Unicellular** organisms (bacteria, yeast) are one cell; **multicellular** ones (plants, fish, birds, humans) have millions of cells working together."]),
 ("table", ["Level", "Meaning", "Example"], [
   ["Cell", "Basic structural and functional unit", "A nerve cell"],
   ["Tissue", "Group of similar cells doing similar work", "Muscle tissue"],
   ["Organ", "Different tissues organised for a function", "Lungs"],
   ["Organ system", "Several organs working together", "Respiratory system: nasal pores, nasal cavity, trachea, lungs"]]),
 ("h2", "9.2 How to Study Cells?"),
 ("ul", ["The human eye can separate two points only if they are about **0.1 mm** apart (seen from the near point, 25 cm). This is its **limit of resolution**. Most cells are smaller, so tools are needed.", "**Robert Hooke (1665)** first saw cells, in a thin slice of **cork**, with a self-made microscope of 200–300X. The box-like compartments he named 'cells'.", "A **light microscope** uses convex lenses: an **objective** lens (e.g. 10X, 40X) and an **eyepiece**. Parts: eyepiece, body tube, objective lens, coarse and fine adjustment knobs, stage, mirror, handle, base.", "**Electron microscopes** use a beam of electrons instead of light and reveal details at the nanometre scale (1 nm = one-billionth of a metre); a scanning electron microscope image can show structures like the **stomata** of a Colocasia leaf.", "Microscopes have improved in **resolution** (clarity), **contrast** (brightness difference between parts) and **magnification**."]),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "Total magnification = eyepiece power × objective power (10X × 10X = 100X). &nbsp; Size of a cell = diameter of field of view (µm) ÷ number of cells along the diameter. &nbsp; 1 mm = 1000 µm."),
 ("steps", [("Activity 9.1 — estimate cell size", "Place a mm ruler on the stage and measure the diameter of the field of view (say 5 mm = 5000 µm)."), ("Count cells", "Replace it with an onion-peel slide and count the cells across the diameter in a straight line (say 25)."), ("Calculate", "Cell size = 5000 µm ÷ 25 = 200 µm. At 100X it appears 100 times larger.")]),
 ("h2", "9.3 Cell Membrane and Osmosis"),
 ("p", "The **cell membrane (plasma membrane)** is the thin outer boundary of every cell. It protects the contents, gives the cell its individuality and is **selectively permeable** — it lets some substances in or out and blocks others (as in the alveoli of lungs, where O<sub>2</sub> and CO<sub>2</sub> cross)."),
 ("steps", [("Activity — potato in water and solution", "Cut a potato into two equal pieces, weigh each, and place one in plain water and the other in 20% salt or sugar solution for about an hour."), ("Observe", "The piece in water **swells** (weight rises); the piece in solution **shrinks** (weight falls)."), ("Infer", "Water moves through the membrane, but sugar and salt do not. Equal size and initial weights are needed for a fair comparison.")]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Diffusion**: net movement of particles from higher to lower concentration (no membrane needed). **Osmosis**: diffusion of **water** across a selectively permeable membrane, from a dilute solution (more water) to a concentrated one (less water) until the concentrations equalise. Plant roots absorb soil water by osmosis."),
 ("table", ["Outside solution", "Condition", "Effect on cell"], [
   ["**Isotonic**", "Solute outside = solute inside", "No net water movement; size unchanged"],
   ["**Hypotonic**", "Solute outside &lt; solute inside (dilute)", "Water enters; cell swells"],
   ["**Hypertonic**", "Solute outside &gt; solute inside (concentrated)", "Water leaves; cell shrinks"]]),
 ("h3", "Structure of the membrane — fluid-mosaic model"),
 ("ul", ["Very thin: about **7–10 nm**; made of **lipids and proteins**.", "A **lipid bilayer** (two layers of fat molecules with water-loving heads outward and water-repelling tails inward) with **proteins embedded** in it.", "Molecules can move sideways, flip and rotate, so it is **fluid**; they are arranged like tiles of a **mosaic**; proteins act as **gatekeepers** for passage."]),
 ("h2", "9.4 Cell Wall"),
 ("ul", ["Plants, fungi and bacteria have an extra layer, the **cell wall**, outside the membrane. Animal cells lack it.", "Plants cannot move, so the wall lets them withstand wind and rain, keeps leaves and flowers firm and holds the plant upright.", "It is rigid but **freely permeable** to water and dissolved minerals, helping roots absorb nutrients. Plant walls are mainly **cellulose** (many glucose units); cellulose in our diet is **roughage**."]),
 ("table", ["Observation", "Plant cell (onion / Rhoeo)", "Animal cell (cheek)"], [
   ["Shape", "Box-like, regularly arranged", "Irregular, flexible"],
   ["In 20% sugar solution", "Outline unchanged; inner content shrinks as the membrane pulls away from the wall", "Whole cell shrinks"],
   ["Reason", "Rigid cell wall holds shape", "No cell wall"]]),
 ("p", "Slide method: peel of onion or Rhoeo stained with **safranin**; cheek cells scraped with a toothpick and stained with **methylene blue**. Flexibility of animal cells supports movement of their tissues."),
 ("h2", "9.5 The Cell Interior — A Coordinated System"),
 ("p", "Most cells have a membrane, a semi-fluid **cytoplasm** and a **nucleus**. The cytoplasm holds **organelles** (mostly visible only with electron microscopes) and **inclusions** such as starch in plants or crystals of calcium oxalate or silica. In eukaryotes a network of fibres, the **cytoskeleton**, gives support, shape, movement and internal transport."),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Prokaryotic cell", "Eukaryotic cell"], [
   ["Meaning", "Pro = primitive, karyon = nucleus", "Eu = true, karyon = nucleus"],
   ["Nucleus", "Absent; DNA is a single circular molecule in the **nucleoid** (no membrane)", "Well-defined, membrane-bound"],
   ["Membrane-bound organelles", "Absent; activities happen in cytoplasm", "Present"],
   ["Typical diameter", "1–10 µm", "10–100 µm"],
   ["Number of cells", "Usually unicellular", "Unicellular or multicellular"],
   ["Example", "Bacteria", "Plant and animal cells"]]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "Viruses, viroids and prions are **acellular** infectious agents, too small for a light microscope. A virus is genetic material in a protein coat; a viroid has no coat; a prion is a misfolded protein with no genetic material."),
 ("h3", "Why organelles? — the cell as a tiny factory"),
 ("p", "Separate organelles carry out different jobs at the same time: building materials, removing waste and providing energy."),
 ("table", ["Organelle", "Structure", "Function"], [
   ["**Nucleus**", "Double-layered nuclear membrane with pores; nucleolus (makes ribosome subunits); chromatin / chromosomes", "Controls the cell; holds DNA (genes) for inheritance"],
   ["**Ribosomes**", "Tiny; free in cytoplasm or attached to ER", "Protein synthesis ('protein factories')"],
   ["**Rough ER (RER)**", "Network continuous with nuclear envelope; ribosomes on surface", "Protein synthesis and secretion (e.g. pancreatic gland cells)"],
   ["**Smooth ER (SER)**", "No ribosomes", "Makes and stores fats (lipids) and hormones"],
   ["**Golgi apparatus**", "Stacks of flattened sacs; found in 1898 by Camillo Golgi in barn-owl nerve cells", "Modifies, sorts and packs proteins/lipids into vesicles ('post office'); forms lysosomes"],
   ["**Lysosomes**", "Single-membrane sacs full of enzymes", "Digest wastes, worn-out parts and foreign matter ('clean-up'); sperm lysosomal enzymes help penetrate the egg"],
   ["**Mitochondria**", "Double membrane; outer smooth, inner folded into **cristae**; own DNA and ribosomes", "Cellular respiration; release energy stored as **ATP** ('powerhouse')"],
   ["**Plastids**", "Double membrane in plants (chloroplast has stroma and chlorophyll discs)", "Make and store food"],
   ["**Vacuole**", "Large central one in plants with **cell sap**; small in animals", "Stores water, minerals, sugars and wastes; keeps cell firm (turgor)"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Chromatin** is the entangled thread-like DNA material in a non-dividing cell; it condenses into rod-like **chromosomes** (DNA + proteins) just before division. A **gene** is a functional segment of DNA.", "Mature **RBCs** have no nucleus (enucleate): more room for haemoglobin and so more oxygen carried, but they cannot divide or repair themselves and live only about **120 days**.", "Mitochondria and chloroplasts have their **own DNA and ribosomes**, like bacteria, hinting at a shared evolutionary history.", "**Wilting**: when a plant lacks water the vacuole loses water, cells lose firmness and the plant droops."]),
 ("table", ["Plastid", "Pigment", "Role"], [
   ["**Chloroplast**", "Green chlorophyll", "Absorbs sunlight for photosynthesis; sugars and starch stored in stroma"],
   ["**Chromoplast**", "Yellow, orange or red", "Colours flowers and fruits to attract pollinators and seed dispersers"],
   ["**Leucoplast**", "Colourless", "Stores starch, oils or proteins (e.g. in potato and taro)"]]),
 ("table", ["Structure", "Plant cell", "Animal cell", "Bacterial cell"], [
   ["Cell membrane", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes"], ["Cell wall", "Yes", "No", "Yes"], ["Cytoplasm", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes"],
   ["True nucleus", "Yes", "Yes", "No (nucleoid)"], ["Membrane-bound organelles", "Yes", "Yes", "No"],
   ["Plastids / large vacuole", "Yes", "No (small vacuoles)", "No"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "Venter's 2010 experiment: DNA of *Mycoplasma mycoides* was synthesised in the lab and inserted into a related bacterium whose own DNA was removed. The cell then grew and divided as per the new DNA, showing that **DNA controls the cell** — but only the DNA was synthetic, not the whole cell."),
 ("h2", "9.6 Cell Growth and Division"),
 ("p", "The body grows not because cells get bigger without limit but because cells **divide**. Cell division forms new cells from pre-existing ones for **growth, repair and reproduction**. About 1% of body cells (hundreds of billions) are replaced daily. The **onion root tip** is a good place to see dividing cells (stages differ in appearance)."),
 ("steps", [("Prepare roots", "Grow onion roots in water for 5–6 days; cut 2–3 cm tips and fix in aceto-alcohol (acetic acid : ethanol = 1 : 3) for 24 hours; store in 70% ethanol."), ("Soften and stain", "Place a root on a slide, add dilute HCl for 10–15 min, rinse, then stain with aceto-carmine for 5–10 min and warm gently."), ("Squash and observe", "Cut the tip, add a coverslip, press gently with the thumb and view: cells show different stages of division.")]),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Mitosis", "Meiosis"], [
   ["Occurs in", "Body (somatic) cells", "Only reproductive organs: testes/ovaries; anthers/ovary of flowers"],
   ["Divisions", "One", "Two, one after the other"],
   ["Daughter cells", "Two", "Four"],
   ["Chromosome number", "Same as parent", "**Halved** in each gamete"],
   ["Genetic nature", "Identical to parent", "Varied"],
   ["Purpose", "Growth, repair, maintenance, asexual reproduction", "Gametes for sexual reproduction; diversity"]]),
 ("ul", ["Every human starts as one fertilised egg that divides by mitosis into trillions of cells. Prokaryotes also divide; eukaryotes follow a controlled **cell cycle**.", "In **fertilisation** two gametes fuse and restore the original chromosome number; this variation is why children resemble but differ from parents.", "**Errors**: faulty mitosis → uncontrolled division, tumours, abnormal chromosome numbers; faulty meiosis → genetic disorders, developmental problems, early pregnancy loss, reduced fertility.", "**Cell culture**: cells grown outside the body in nutrient medium under right temperature, pH, moisture and sterility, used for studying cells and making biochemicals, food, medicines and vaccines."]),
 ("h2", "9.7 Cell Theory"),
 ("table", ["Scientist", "Year", "Contribution"], [["Matthias Schleiden", "1838", "All plants are made of cells"], ["Theodor Schwann", "1839", "All animals are made of cells"], ["Rudolf Virchow", "1855", "New cells arise only from pre-existing cells"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Cell Theory**: (1) all living organisms are made of one or more cells; (2) the cell is the basic unit of structure and function; (3) all cells arise from pre-existing cells."),
 ("ul", ["Haberlandt (1902) proposed **totipotency**: a living plant cell can grow into a complete plant, founding plant tissue culture.", "Every cell has a definite **life span**. Many animal cells stop dividing on touching neighbours (**contact inhibition**). **Cancer cells** lose this control and form **tumours** (benign or malignant; malignant ones invade and spread). Plant cells show no contact inhibition because of their rigid walls.", "**Programmed cell death (PCD)** is genetically regulated destruction of cells, needed for development, quality control and immunity (e.g. it removes cells between embryonic fingers; without it hands would be webbed)."]),
 ("h2", "9.8 Quick Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
   ("Who first observed cells and how?", "Robert Hooke in 1665, in a slice of cork under a self-made microscope; he named the box-like compartments cells."),
   ("What is osmosis? Why does a potato piece shrink in strong salt solution?", "Osmosis is movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from dilute to concentrated solution; the strong solution is hypertonic, so water leaves the cells."),
   ("Differentiate isotonic, hypotonic and hypertonic solutions.", "Isotonic: equal solute outside and inside, no change; hypotonic: less solute outside, cell swells; hypertonic: more solute outside, cell shrinks."),
   ("Why does a plant cell not burst or shrink in size in a solution?", "The rigid permeable cell wall holds its shape; only the inner content changes."),
   ("Explain the fluid-mosaic model.", "A lipid bilayer with embedded proteins; molecules move sideways (fluid) and lie like mosaic tiles; proteins help substances pass."),
   ("Compare prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.", "Prokaryotes: nucleoid, no membrane-bound organelles, 1–10 µm, usually unicellular; eukaryotes: true nucleus and organelles, 10–100 µm."),
   ("Why are mitochondria called the powerhouse of the cell?", "They carry out cellular respiration and store the energy as ATP, the cell's energy currency."),
   ("Differentiate RER and SER.", "RER has ribosomes and makes proteins; SER has none and makes fats and hormones."),
   ("Distinguish chloroplast, chromoplast and leucoplast.", "Green photosynthetic pigment; yellow/orange/red pigments for colour; colourless, stores food."),
   ("Why do RBCs lack a nucleus and what is the effect?", "More space for haemoglobin and oxygen; but they cannot repair or divide, living about 120 days."),
   ("Compare mitosis and meiosis.", "Mitosis gives two identical cells with the same chromosome number for growth and repair; meiosis gives four cells with half the number, forming gametes and variation."),
   ("State the Cell Theory.", "All organisms consist of cells; the cell is the basic unit; all cells come from pre-existing cells."),
   ("What is the link between wilting and vacuoles?", "Without water the vacuole shrinks, pressure falls and cells lose firmness, so the plant droops."),
 ])],
 takeaways=["The **cell** is the basic structural and functional unit of life; Hooke saw cells in **cork (1665)**.", "The membrane is **selectively permeable** (fluid-mosaic); **osmosis** is diffusion of water, giving **isotonic, hypotonic, hypertonic** effects.", "**Cell wall** (cellulose) occurs in plants, fungi and bacteria; **prokaryotes** have a nucleoid, **eukaryotes** a true nucleus and organelles.", "Learn organelle functions: nucleus (control), ribosome (proteins), ER, Golgi (packing), lysosome (digestion), **mitochondria (ATP)**, plastids (food), vacuole (storage).", "**Mitosis**: 2 identical cells; **meiosis**: 4 cells with half the chromosomes.", "**Cell Theory** (Schleiden, Schwann, Virchow); cancer is uncontrolled division, while **PCD** removes cells in an orderly way."])
